#edb/ref 17592186365274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Unknown Value" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179693 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186365316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186489224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179715 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1045 "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1050 1200.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179847 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4cb8dd70-6479-5b35-b723-3890d781528c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186178239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(1-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=3\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e70a7267-ff55-5f84-86c7-c840d8b569be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180095 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/75/75ef4755be2965b674fc7a5440ff05bb3f37d70507941eb48eeae554fcb2d538.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(1-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(1-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1-x) & =0 \\\\ 1-x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $\\frac{k}{2}>0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{k}{2}<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(1-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =k(0-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ when $x=\\frac{3}{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(3)^{2} & =k(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2})+3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =\\frac{3}{8}k+3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{8}k & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-4{x}^{2}+6 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{8x-4{x}^{2}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{8x-4{x}^{2}+6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(1)=\\sqrt{8(1)-4(1)^{2}+6}=\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has velocity $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $9\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0e04fbd-7109-56e7-b82c-58a2adaa4ded" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180098 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/b0/b052a65fb0dbcc14a5d359b693beafdf5da3f4bbe1e10f006c315e8168340d5c.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k\\sqrt{x}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (k\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(0)^{3/2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=6\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=9\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}{x}^{3/2}+18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6\\sqrt{6})^{2} & =\\frac{2k}{3}(9)^{3/2}+18 \\\\ 108 & =18k+18 \\\\ 18k & =90 \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180416 #edb/ref 1061 33784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec16918e-d414-592f-ad99-94fed4da6b99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180417 #edb/ref 1061 33785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3cafc43-ac5d-5524-9bd0-e3b82ec62fd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1061 35399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "28c57294-3b4c-56e0-8cac-331a28850e07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180453 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1061 35400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a2b4d25-22b2-5303-9d34-924730dd694c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180454 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180703 #edb/ref 1064 "We've seen how to calculate a particle's velocity and displacement when its acceleration is a function of *displacement.* In this lesson, we'll use this knowledge to compute various properties of a particle in motion.  \n\nSuppose a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. Its displacement from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ and its acceleration is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ \n\n![](images/52/5222b12a7f08a33dc981ac95bd717cce945e1d2e3565fb3631f108fd0f307a12.png)\n\nFurthermore, suppose $P$ has velocity $2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $\\sqrt{3}\\,\\rm{m}.$ \n\nLet's use this information to determine at which values of $x$ the particle is instantaneously at rest.\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int 2x \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2+ C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=\\sqrt 3\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ We find the displacement of the particle when $P$ is instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180703 #edb/ref 1065 5769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180703 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dcdbb7a7-457c-5386-9a73-ae4d9da40f89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180703 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $kx\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "850e5b75-4192-55dd-95c1-60411426a4e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365267 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/cd/cd42eb74e5bba02951118c349a1b25f7193c39aa53f6504403918dfa984eb2c2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $kx\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-kx.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-kx.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =-\\frac{k}{2}(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=18-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=3\\sqrt{2}\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =18-\\frac{k}{2}{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =18-\\frac{k}{2}(3\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\ 18-9k & =0 \\\\ 9k & =18 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365268 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a4d2807e-e4a2-58a8-bf6a-268785e8822d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365269 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365269 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365269 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6dde8744-6fdc-5e2d-b736-074a3c935944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365270 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365270 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365270 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e60c10f-bfb0-5906-b010-ebfe71f74caa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365271 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365271 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365271 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e931fb28-3c65-5cdd-bd58-88282355e37a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365272 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365272 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365272 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "dd1e58b1-199d-5fb2-88e7-6318356fb651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365273 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365273 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365273 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59060e22-55d4-5810-9281-aa0ce058e469" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(6-3x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a4140db-1f5a-5bb9-b5b9-f7487b2da373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365281 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/dd/dd8eecba814fda9c628dc4c8a3853805a31e57f953dd9bf26a6fa79f5c700788.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(6-3x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6-3x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $3>0$ | $0$ | $-3<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6-3x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (6-3x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =6(0)-\\frac{3}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =12x-3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+12x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-3{x}^{2}+12x+2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=\\sqrt{-3(2)^{2}+12(2)+2}=\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365282 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 175